When I began improving `git-subtrees`, I thought I was comparing commands. Should a monorepo use submodules, `git subtree`, or `git-subrepo`? Which one has the nicer pull workflow? Which one produces the cleanest history?

The more useful question turned out to be: **where does the synchronization state live?**

That choice determines whether a tool survives rebases, squash merges, directory moves, and years of ordinary maintenance.

## Three places to store state

A submodule stores its essential state as a gitlink in the parent repository. The checked-out directory is a separate repository, and the parent records the exact commit it expects. This is explicit and efficient, but it exposes users to detached HEADs and a two-repository workflow.

`git subtree` stores no dedicated state file. Instead, it encodes relationships in commit history. Squashed pulls create synthetic commits and merges that later commands inspect. This keeps the working tree pleasantly ordinary, but the meaning is implicit and fragile when history is rewritten.

`git-subrepo` writes metadata into the worktree. That was the architectural clue I needed. Unfortunately, its metadata includes a monorepo commit ID called `parent`. After a feature branch is squash-merged, that commit may no longer exist in the current history. The state is explicit, but one field points at an unstable identity.

## The failure that changed the design

My normal workflow squash-merges every pull request. I reproduced a small case: pull a subrepository on a feature branch, squash-merge it, then try the next push from `main`. `git-subrepo` could no longer find its recorded parent and stopped.

The same experiment exposed a different bug in my existing `git-subtrees` tool. Its synchronization merge disappeared during the squash. A purely local change was then classified as diverged, and recovery could export an upstream commit twice.

Both failures came from treating a commit in rewriteable monorepo history as permanent state.

## A state file without a monorepo SHA

The design that followed uses a small file inside every synchronized folder. It records:

- the upstream repository URL;
- the upstream commit whose tree the folder last matched;
- an optional upstream default branch.

It records no monorepo commit ID. The local boundary can be derived as the newest commit that changed the state file. If a rebase or squash merge rewrites that commit, the new history still contains a boundary with the same meaning.

Putting the file inside the folder also makes a move unsurprising: `git mv` moves configuration and content together. When history is exported upstream, the state file is omitted.

The lesson extends beyond Git tooling. When designing synchronization, ask which identities are stable in the operations users actually perform. A state file is not automatically robust, and history is not automatically a database. The durable design records only facts owned by the other repository and derives the rest from the current one.
